In a system of particles, the force on the $i$-th particle splits as
$
  bold(F_i) = bold(F_i^"ext") + sum_(j != i) bold(F_(i j)),
$
and the third law makes the internal contributions cancel in pairs:
$
  sum_i sum_(j != i) bold(F_(i j)) = sum_(i < j) (bold(F_(i j)) + bold(F_(j i))) = bold(0).
$
Hence $bold(dot(P)) = sum_i bold(F_i^"ext")$, so the total momentum of a system is conserved in the absence of external forces. When internal forces are derived from pair potentials $V_(i j)$, the third law also requires $V_(i j) = V_(j i)$.
